I suspect that this is part of it. I used to think it was practically 
impossible to die once I'd made it past the quest; I'd had four or five 
ascensions, and only died once past Castle (tackling Astral with not 
good enough AC).

That was before my tourist got zapped with a wand of striking, after 
having found the vibrating square, while recrossing the Castle bridge; 
before my Samurai got slimed in Gehennom (didn't know the remedy for 
that); before my healer got drowned by an electric eel on the quest 
(didn't know they were strong enough to pull you in); before another 
Samurai got death-rayed on the Quest (had gotten down to Medusa--which 
had a hostile Titan--without finding a source of MR or reflection); and 
before my Ranger, happily plowing through Gehennom with no problems, got 
killed by Asmodeus with three successive cold attacks because he didn't 
realize he had had cold resistance by wielding Frost Brand and not 
intrinsically (not for lack of eating winter wolf and blue jelly corpses).

Not to say that all of these deaths were unavoidable, but I really had 
thought I was beyond that level of play.

I think that the time and attention you have to devote to the game make 
a big difference as well. If you have the luxury of being able to 
immerse yourself in it for days at a time, it's easier than if you have 
to play a few minutes or an hour here or there.

